ey were studying some  quite distinct medical problem ok so the  genes that we ended up identifying the  human counterparts of those genes are  now being used in the biotechnology and  pharmaceutical industry  raised as novel drug targets to  intervene with the process of programmed  cell death for example if you could  block the activity of a killer gene you  might be able to treat a disease in  which there is too much programmed cell  death a disease like retinal  degeneration or liver disorders and so  on and so forth conversely if you can  block a gene that is a protector you  might intervene with a disease in which  there is too little cell death because  you would be activating cell death and  so in this context I actually have  another hat besides the head I were  across the street I've been very  involved in the biotechnology and to  some extent pharmaceutical industries in  an advisory capacity I founded a company  based upon these findings with  programmed cell death the company was  purchased by a major pharmaceutical  company and two of the drugs that we  developed are in active pursuit one for  treating liver disease and the second  for treating certain human cancers okay  so that takes from the study of a worm  the biology all the way through to new  findings relevant to basic biology the  biology of a worm to human biology to  human disease to to drug discovery and  drug development now so far we haven't  cured anybody but we haven't killed  anybody yet either so we're list on on  the right route okay so those are those  are some of the i would say bare-bones  aspects of of the science at least part  of the science that was recognized in  Stockholm and let me just finish these  introductory comments with a few what I  would say our messages from my  experiences related to to the things  I've told you about first of all science  can very much be  a team endeavor myself I was very lucky  to have outstanding teachers and mentors  so for the young folks i would say  choose your teachers wisely you want  good teachers because they really  motivate you and they help you in  addition I've been very lucky to have  outstanding researchers working with me  outstanding post doctoral scientists and  graduate students and undergraduates and  it was really these trainees who  performed most of the experiments that  ended up being recognized in Stockholm a  second comment the research that I've  described that we did was absolutely  basic scientific research when I began  neither the generality nor the potential  application of our studies was at all  clear in fact this limited C elegans was  completely obscure at this point I was  advised not to work on it because it  would be a scientific dead-end genetic  studies are often highly abstract I  didn't target any disease and I didn't  have any idea if the research we did  would be quote relevant to any organism  other than a worm that lives in the soil  nonetheless our studies established  mechanisms that appear to be universal  among animals and also have provided the  basis for a new understanding of human  biology human disease and new approaches  to human therapeutics and so I think  there's a very important message here  basic research discovery oriented  research can be very fundamentally  important in a variety of ways first of  all it's interesting it's intellectually  satisfying and I think that's what  drives many basic research  researchers but secondly it's from  search such basic research that  unexpected discoveries come and it's  these unexpected discoveries that are  the big leaves if you're always looking  under the lamppost and always trying to  make this next step focused on something  very specific you may make that step but  it won't be as much as a surprise as you  will by doing something that is much  more exploratory basic research is  really the driver of scientific  knowledge and having said that I want to  turn a little bit to policy I mentioned  that I have a second hat that i  sometimes wear in the commercial world I  have a third hat that i sometimes wear  inside the beltway I go to Washington  and I speak with policymakers there I've  met with members of Congress I've  delivered a what's called a  Congressional Caucus about biological  research and what I try to tell people  there is about the importance of basic  research and also the importance of  federal government funding for basic  research if all of the research that is  funded first research funding is  important but secondly there must be a  portfolio of research interests if all  of the research is funded is targeted to  very specific areas then these  out-of-the-box kinds of discoveries will  never come and I would argue progress  would be much slower I don't think today  we would have the understanding of the  aspects of biology that are fundamental  to many many diseases if each disease  had been studied into and onto itself  without the kinds of basic explorations  that I was very fortunate to be able to  do initially only with funding from the  federal  government from the National Institutes  of Health the federal government must  support this work and thirdly I come  back to something I commented on earlier  for particularly the young folks don't  be afraid don't be afraid of trying  something new and also don't be afraid  if you really believe in something to go  against advice you were given I was told  I was headed for a scientific dead end  by working on this obscure organism and  I think my scientific life to date has  been anything other than that so let me  stop these introductory comments at this  point and turn it over to questions  comments and whatever well thank you  very much indeed so we're going to use  the rest of our time for Question and  Answer I take a couple from here then  I'm going to hope to fit in at least one  from our friends in Cairo so please use  the microphones if you're going to ask  and I see a first question