somes and our  chromosomes contain two basic components  DNA and protein very different kinds of  molecules okay and the protein the major  protein ah but not by any means the only  protein associated with DNA  our proteins called histones ok so the  histones basically the the what happens  is you get the DNA of double helix that  wraps around a set of histones and then  that whole business is folded up and  then those folded up things are folded  up again and all of that is packed into  our chromosomes now the histones and the  DNA can be modified in ways that are  distinct from mutations for example a  certain chemical moiety a methyl group  can be added to the DNA there are a  variety of chemical modifications that  can be made to the histones and these  modifications can alter the DNA histone  chromosome structure locally in ways  that affect which genes are expressed in  which genes are not expressed so that  epigenetics basically involves a series  of alterations either to DNA or to  histones or to the arrangement of  histones and particularly groups of  histones known as nucleosomes along the  DNA and those differences can be  inherited so it's a whole second  universe of heritability and sometimes  that heritability can be from generation  to generation parent to child and in  some cases it's heritability from cell  to cell so if you think for example  about again the single-celled fertilized  egg dividing and dividing to make all  the cells in our bodies one of the  things that's happening there is that  there are these modifications of DNA and  histones that get inherited so the  patterns that you'll see in a set of  nerve cells will be different from what  you see in a set of blood cells will be  different from what you see in a set of  muscle cells okay  and all of those are examples of  epigenetic inheritance okay so that's  epigenetic and now I have to apologize i  forgot the question just use the mic if  you could because no one else can hope  is there methylation involved in the  switching on and off of the genes  responsible for apoptosis as there is  for other epigenetic phenomenon okay so  epigenetic phenomena as I've just  alluded to our major aspects of ways in  which genes are controlled but not the  only aspects and there are examples in  mammals where there are indications that  there is epigenetic regulation of some  of the genes that I've alluded to but I  would say it's still very early days we  have found hints in the worm of such  regulation so that we know that some of  the genes that are involved in  epigenetic regulation can also regulate  indirectly aspects of program cell death  but we don't know how we haven't figured  that out at all so the answer is I'm  sure going to be yes but at this point  it's still a bit in the future thank you  okay I'm going to try and take a  question now if I may from our friends  in Cairo if they're listening and can  hear us do you have a question for  professor Horvitz please  people are asking about his basic  research for developing countries like  Egypt and his input on how sectors in  and in particular conclusion to act the  best one is when cancer we just as a  quick aside yes so some questions about  the professor Horvitz your role in  arguing for the role of basic research  in the developing country context first  I think okay that's an excellent  question and one that I think is very  important so the argument is sometimes  made that the wealthy country should be  the ones who spend the money on basic  research and the countries that have  more limited resources should focus on  problems particularly problems in  agriculture and health that are specific  to those countries and my feeling about  that is is that it's not quite so simple  and the reason is that the quality of  science that is done is driven by the  quality of scientists who are doing it  and that quality is very much dependent  upon the sophistication and experience  of the researchers so 