o themselves and so as we  think about trying to treat cancers we  have to remember that there are a  multiplicity of diseases and each may  need some aspects of different approach  having said that the science is getting  better there are some cancers  particularly cancers of young people  where there have been very powerful  cures that have developed over recent  years there are other cancers that are  fundamentally still intractable what the  field of cancer biology is doing today  is hoping to better delineate the  different cancers understand the  biological basis of the different  cancers and develop drugs that can be  useful in one or more of these k  is at the same time learning the phrases  to fractionate patients to understand  for a single patient what disease that  patient really has and therefore which  drugs are most likely to be useful and  which drugs are likely in fact in some  cases to be harmful so the knowledge is  progressing the problem is not easy and  again there's a multiplicity of problems  and I i would say that there is progress  being made that is much slower than  everybody wishes it would be a but with  the proper resource there will be more  progress I cannot begin to give an  answer to when cancer will be cured the  only thing I can tell you is that when a  recent director of the National Cancer  Institute made an announcement that  cancer would be cured within 10 years of  the field of cancer biology jumped up  and said you can't say that yeah thank  you that's a question down here nope  your own ah what is it like to win a  Nobel Prize where you were when you how  the news or reaction or does it change  your walk or life or whatnot right so so  the first answer I would say is does it  change your life and the answer is yes  and I was amazed the day it happened to  think that now everybody I knew knew  somebody who had a Nobel Prize  um where was I and also I lad did I know  ahead of time so i was aware ahead of  time that it was possible i had been  aware that people had nominated me and  in fact MIT is not unaggressive in  informing people that they are at least  thinking about it so for a number of  years prior to the 2002 announcement i  got phone calls from the MIT press  office asking me where i would be on the  day of the prize announcement which by  the way is always the the first monday  in october for medicine okay 2002 I  didn't get such a call so i figured MIT  knew something i was no longer on the  list it wasn't going to happen so so be  it where i was actually well it depends  what what source of information you use  if you use the new york times as a  source of information i was with my  family vacationing in the French Alps if  you know the life of a faculty member at  MIT is unlikely and think about October  when I teach my course in genetics you  could suspect that that couldn't have  been the whole story so where I was with  my family indeed was the French Alps we  had gone to France for the weekend  because our young daughter had had a  French au pair and this all pair was  getting married so we went for the  wedding the wedding was on saturday we  went to the wedding on saturday we spent  time with the family on sunday and we  took off monday to spend extra because  it was after the wedding so we would  have some time with our are basically  our daughter is the way we feel about  her and her new husband so we were there  monday one day beyond the weekend  vacationing in the French Alps okay now  on that morning on monday morning my  daughter wanted to go out to the lake  she had heard about from her or pair  where she could catch crayfish now if  you know the elves in October it's not  so warm so we went out there it was  freezing cold my daughter who was nine  at the time went out didn't matter the  cold waited in collected a few  crustaceans everybody else was frozen  and we went back to the home of the  author's parents and when we walked in  her father who speaks no E